Description: Show restored windows without activating them and open a new window for every launch.
 Ptyxis restores its session by presenting every restored window. GTK spends the activation token of the launch on
 the first window it shows, so with a valid token, as from GNOME Shell's dock, mutter focuses that restored window
 and switches to its workspace. The launch itself opens no new window: activation and the new-window action only
 restore the session at a cold start, and activation presents an existing window while Ptyxis runs.
 .
 Restored windows are now shown with GTK's automatic startup notification turned off, so they return to their places
 without being activated. Activation, the new-window action, --new-window and opening files then always open a new
 window, presented after the restored ones, which receives the activation token. A tab requested with --tab or
 --tab-with-profile goes into a new window when the session was restored just now at a cold start, since the current
 window may be on another workspace, and into the current window while Ptyxis runs. get_current_window() no longer
 returns a hidden window GTK created for restored window state when it is the active window.
 .
 Mutter focuses a window it shows when the window maps without an activation token, and it shows a restored window
 only after the window drew its first frame, several of them at once in an order of its own, so a restored window
 with a restored screen could be shown after the new window and receive the focus. The new window is therefore presented
 once mutter showed each restored window on a monitor, or 250 ms after the last of them drew its first frame, as one
 on another workspace is not shown, and after a second at the latest.
